## "Let's think step by step"

A model writes one token at a time, and every token it writes becomes context for the next (Ch 11).
If it has to give the answer as its very first token, all the working has to happen inside one
forward pass. If it writes the working out first, a <GlossaryPopover term="chain-of-thought">chain of thought</GlossaryPopover>,
each step can build on the ones before. Reasoning models are trained to do this (Ch 25); with other
models, you can ask.

Asking works on large models. Showing a few worked examples improved math word problems,<Cite id="wei2022" />
and simply adding "Let's think step by step" raised one large model's accuracy on a set of arithmetic
word problems from 17.7% to 78.7%.<Cite id="kojima2022" />

## A small model, three ways

Here are the puzzles from Ch 25, put to Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct with three different endings:

<StepsTally />

For this small model, the steps didn't raise the score: each way got 4 of the 6 right, but not the
same 4. Asked for just the answer, it fell for the classic trap and said the ball costs 10 cents.
Thinking step by step fixed the apples but talked itself into "0.2 minutes" for the widgets. Big
gains from step-by-step prompting were found in much larger models; small ones can follow the form
without the reasoning holding up.

## Formats are for programs

The last ending asked for the answer on a final line, "Answer: …", so that a program could read it.
The model did that only once in six tries. A harness that parses replies (Ch 16) can't assume the
format was followed: it has to check, and ask again or fall back when it wasn't. Asking for a format
makes the reply easier to use, not guaranteed.

<RecapCard>

- Writing the working out gives each step something to build on; it helped large models a lot.
- On a small model the effect was mixed: the same score, with different mistakes.
- Ask for a fixed format when a program reads the reply, and check that it was followed.

</RecapCard>

**Next question:** if wording matters this much, how much can harmless changes move the answer?
